 egin{proof} Since $C$ is line-free, it has at least one extreme
  point. Let $c$ be one such extreme point. Assume that for every
  sequence $x^{
u}$ with~${x^{
u} \in \conv \vertices P^{
u}}$ there
  exists a $\gamma > 0$, such that~${
orm{x^{
u}-c} > \gamma}$ for
  infinitely many $
u$. Then, without loss of generality, there exists
  one such sequence such that~$
orm{x^{
u}-c} > \gamma$ for every
  $
u$ and, since $C \subseteq P^{
u}$, $c=x^{
u}+r^{
u}$ for some
  $r^{
u} \in ecc{P^{
u}}$. By Lemma ef{LBS} it holds that
  $
orm{x^{
u}}$ and $
orm{r^{
u}}$ are bounded. Then there exist
  subsequences $x^{
u_k}$, $r^{
u_k}$ such that
  \[ x^{
u_k} ightarrow x \in C, \quad r^{
u_k} ightarrow r \in
    ecc{C}. \] Note that $r 
eq 0$, because $
orm{r^{
u}} > \gamma$
  for all $
u$. Finally,
  \[ c=x+r= rac{1}{2}\left( x+2r ight) +  rac{1}{2} x. \] This is
  a contradiction to $c$ being an extreme point of $C$.
\end{proof}

 egin{proof}
  By Theorem ef{TPKCHD} we must show that there exist $c \in \R^n$
  and $r_0 \geq 0$ such that for all $r \geq r_0$ it holds $\haus{P^{
u} \cap B_r(c)}{C \cap B_r(c)}
  ightarrow 0$. Let~${r \geq \max_{
u \in
      \mathbb{N}} \varepsilon^{
u}}$ and let $c$ be an extreme point of $C$,
  which exists, because $C$ contains no lines. By Proposition ef{PEP}
  there exists a sequence~${x^{
u} ightarrow c}$ with~${x^{
u} \in
    \conv \vertices P^{
u}}$. Applying the triangle inequality and
  Proposition ef{PHDA} yields
   egin{equation*}
     egin{aligned}
      & & &  \haus{P^{
u} \cap B_r(c)}{C \cap B_r(c)} \
      & \leq & & \haus{P^{
u} \cap B_r(c)}{P^{
u} \cap B_r(x^{
u})}
      \ & & + & \haus{P^{
u} \cap B_r(x^{
u})}{C \cap B_r(x^{
u})}
      \ & & + & \haus{C \cap B_r(x^{
u})}{C \cap B_r(c)} \
      & \leq & & \haus{P^{
u} \cap B_r(c)}{P^{
u} \cap B_r(x^{
u})}
      \ & & + & 2(\varepsilon^{
u}+\delta^{
u}(r+
orm{x^{
u}-v^{
u}}))
      \ & & + & \haus{C \cap B_r(x^{
u})}{C \cap B_r(c)},
   \end{aligned}
 \end{equation*}
 for some $v^{
u} \in \conv \vertices P^{
u}$. The first and third
 term in this sum converge to zero as~${x^{
u} ightarrow c}$. It
 remains to show that~$
orm{x^{
u}-v^{
u}}$ is
 bounded. Since $C \subseteq P^{
u}$, the distance ${\haus{P^{
u}
   \cap B_r(x^{
u})}{C \cap B_r(x^{
u})}}$ is attained as
 $\exc{P^{
u} \cap B_r(x^{
u})}{C \cap B_r(x^{
u})}$. Let the
 supremum be attained by~$p^{
u} \in P^{
u}$. Then $p^{
u} =
   v^{
u} + d^{
u}$ for some~$d^{
u} \in ecc{P^{
u}}$. It holds
 \[ 
orm{p^{
u}-c} \leq 
orm{p^{
u}-x^{
u}} + 
orm{x^{
u}-c}
   \leq r + \max_{
u \in \mathbb{N}} 
orm{x^{
u}-c} < +\infty, \]
 i.e. $v^{
u}+d^{
u} \in B_M(c)$ for some $M \geq 0$. Therefore, the
 sequence $
orm{v^{
u}}$ is bounded according to Lemma
 ef{LBS}. Hence, $
orm{x^{
u}-v^{
u}}$ is also bounded
 and $\haus{P^{
u} \cap B_r(c)}{C \cap B_r(c)} ightarrow 0$,
 which was to be proved.
\end{proof}

